In semi-supervised medical image segmentation, leveraging limited labeled data and substantial unlabeled data is crucial. We propose an innovative, interpolation-based mixing approach, called TIE, to regularization strategy in semi-supervised learning. This method involves creating a mixed label that represents possible variations in segmentation results, then using it to train a network. We demonstrate that TIE can be implemented using multiple ways, including different confidence levels, ensemble methods, and output distributions. Furthermore, we present evidence that TIE yields superior performance compared to existing methods, including VAT and RND, in various medical segmentation datasets, including cardiac, brain, and abdominal scans, as well as 2D and 3D data, following training with either few or moderate labeled samples. We also show that TIE leads to better outcomes when combined with existing methods, resulting in significant improvements over existing techniques. We believe that our findings support the effectiveness of TIE, along with its code and models, are available for public access. 

